  "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 6\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[6, 8, 26], [29, 25, 25], [7, 17, 8], [9, 10, 28], [21, 7, 11], [8, 25, 22]]\n    machine_assignments = [[2, 1, 0], [1, 2, 0], [0, 2, 1], [1, 0, 2], [1, 2, 0], [0, 2, 1]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
  "LLM_description": "Subject: Coordinated scheduling of current production orders across specialized workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized workstations that support a diverse set of customer orders. Each product moves through a defined routing made up of several operations. The routing specifies which workstation is required at each step and how long that operation will take. Because orders share equipment and follow different paths, the way we sequence work on each workstation has a direct impact on lead time, on-time delivery, and overall throughput.\n\nScheduling challenge\nWe must create a feasible shop-floor schedule that assigns start times to every operation across all workstations. The operations compete for limited machine time and must also respect the fixed processing order within each job. Processing times vary by operation, so poorly sequenced work can create bottlenecks on some machines and idle time on others. The key challenge is to coordinate the flow so that machines are well utilized while downstream steps are ready when needed, without violating any process requirements.\n\nOperating rules and constraints\n- Fixed job routing: Operations within each job must be completed in the prescribed order; a subsequent step cannot begin until its predecessor is finished.\n- Machine specificity: Each operation must be executed on its designated workstation only.\n- Known durations: Each operation\u2019s processing time is defined in advance.\n- Machine exclusivity: A workstation can process only one operation at a time.\n- No interruptions: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without pause.\n\nGoal\nBuild a production schedule that completes all jobs in the shortest possible total time (minimizes the overall finish horizon, or makespan) while adhering to the rules above. Achieving this will reduce queues, balance utilization across workstations, and ensure timely completion of the full order set.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 6\n- Number of machines (workstations): 3\n- Job-by-job production schedule (operations must be performed in the listed sequence):\n  Job 6: Op1 on Machine 0 for 8 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 25 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 22 time units\n  Job 5: Op1 on Machine 1 for 21 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 7 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 11 time units\n  Job 3: Op1 on Machine 0 for 7 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 17 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 8 time units\n  Job 1: Op1 on Machine 2 for 6 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 1 for 8 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 26 time units\n  Job 2: Op1 on Machine 1 for 29 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 25 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 25 time units\n  Job 4: Op1 on Machine 1 for 9 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 10 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 2 for 28 time units",
  "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Coordinated scheduling of current production orders across specialized workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized workstations that support a diverse set of customer orders. Each product moves through a defined routing made up of several operations. The routing specifies which workstation is required at each step and how long that operation will take. Because orders share equipment and follow different paths, the way we sequence work on each workstation has a direct impact on lead time, on-time delivery, and overall throughput.\n\nScheduling challenge\nWe must create a feasible shop-floor schedule that assigns start times to every operation across all workstations. The operations compete for limited machine time and must also respect the fixed processing order within each job. Processing times vary by operation, so poorly sequenced work can create bottlenecks on some machines and idle time on others. The key challenge is to coordinate the flow so that machines are well utilized while downstream steps are ready when needed, without violating any process requirements.\n\nOperating rules and constraints\n- Fixed job routing: Operations within each job must be completed in the prescribed order; a subsequent step cannot begin until its predecessor is finished.\n- Machine specificity: Each operation must be executed on its designated workstation only.\n- Known durations: Each operation\u2019s processing time is defined in advance.\n- Machine exclusivity: A workstation can process only one operation at a time.\n- No interruptions: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without pause.\n\nGoal\nBuild a production schedule that completes all jobs in the shortest possible total time (minimizes the overall finish horizon, or makespan) while adhering to the rules above. Achieving this will reduce queues, balance utilization across workstations, and ensure timely completion of the full order set.\n\nANNEX \